    "website": "https://basalam.com/",
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": ["s1"],
    "description": "Basalam is an Iranian online marketplace organized around individual sellers and their digital stalls. Its social-bazaar approach combines product discovery with direct communication between buyers and merchants.",
    "tags": ["E-commerce", "Marketplaces", "Small businesses", "Social commerce", "Regional commerce"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T18:55:38Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T18:55:38Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Basalam: Iran’s Seller-Led Online Bazaar | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Discover Basalam’s digital stalls, buyer–seller communication, regional business origins, and developer ecosystem, with evidence gaps identified."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "overview",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The digital stall as a business model",
        "content": "Basalam brings the idea of a bazaar into an online marketplace. Sellers establish digital stalls, known in Persian as *ghorfeh*, through which they present products and communicate with potential customers. Digiato’s company profile describes this as a social marketplace connecting merchants from across Iran. The emphasis is on identifiable sellers and conversations around products, rather than a shopping experience built only around a centralized catalogue. [s2]\n\nThe company’s own homepage reinforces that positioning by advertising direct contact with people, payment through banking gateways, a money-back guarantee, and online and telephone support. These are platform promises, not independently measured outcomes. Together, however, they show the functions Basalam presents as necessary to make transactions between unfamiliar buyers and sellers possible. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "origins",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Regional origins and a widening product range",
        "content": "Digiato dates the marketplace’s launch to Aban 1395 in Iran’s Solar Hijri calendar and places its principal office in Qom. Its account emphasizes an early interest in small businesses in towns and villages, especially products made outside large industrial supply chains. This provides useful context for Basalam’s identity: regional producers and their stories were part of the proposition, rather than incidental additions to a general retailer. [s2]\n\nThat origin should not be mistaken for a fixed boundary around the business. The inspected homepage displays a much broader selection, including food, beauty products, household items, and other merchandise. It also links to wholesale and organizational purchasing. A reader should therefore distinguish the marketplace’s small-producer origins from the breadth of commerce presented on the later website. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "transaction-relationships",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "The relationships behind Basalam’s social marketplace",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  S[\"Seller\"] -->|\"Operates\"| T[\"Digital stall\"]\n  T -->|\"Displays\"| P[\"Product offer\"]\n  B[\"Buyer\"] -->|\"Discovers\"| P\n  B -->|\"Discusses products with\"| S\n  M[\"Basalam platform\"] -->|\"Hosts\"| T\n  M -->|\"Advertises payment and support services to\"| B\n```",
        "summary": "The diagram separates the seller, the seller’s stall, and the platform hosting it. Its arrows describe product discovery, communication, and advertised services; they do not establish legal liability or a universal shipping arrangement.",
        "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "developer-ecosystem",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Beyond the storefront",
        "content": "Basalam also presents a developer platform called Salam API. An API, or application programming interface, allows software to communicate with a service in a defined way. The inspected developer homepage links to documentation, an API reference, a developer panel, and an app store. It advertises tools for applications, subscriptions, payments, and promotion. These materials indicate an ambition to support software built around the marketplace, although their presence does not establish adoption or commercial success. [s3]\n\nThis distinction matters for understanding potential business relationships. Selling merchandise through a stall and building software for merchants are different activities, with different requirements. The developer website documents the latter as a separate part of the ecosystem. Availability before the editorial cutoff, access conditions, technical reliability, and integration costs still require verification. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "assessment",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What this model helps explain",
        "content": "Basalam is useful for understanding how regional commerce can be organized around merchant identity and conversation. Its historical profile describes direct seller shipment, while its homepage emphasizes communication and platform support. This combination makes the relationship between buyer, merchant, and intermediary central to the business model. Historical descriptions of fees, settlement, or delivery times should not be treated as current terms. [s1][s2]\n\nThe inspected evidence supports an explanation of the model. It does not establish how much additional income sellers earn, whether rural merchants benefit equally, or how consistently disputes are resolved.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      }
    ],
